{
  "image_name": "3a0adc9a6fa89f3e8bf83617e2f9455c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed immune–fibrotic network, likely in pathological tissue, linking germinal center activity to fibroblast activation and collagen deposition. On the left, follicular dendritic cells, T follicular helper cells, and B cells interact within a germinal centre, with BAFF and cytokines such as IL‑4 and IL‑10 supporting B‑cell responses. These B cells differentiate into plasmablasts that produce antibodies, including IgG4, IgE, and IgG1.\n\nIn the surrounding tissue, several CD4-positive T-cell populations, including T helper, regulatory, and cytotoxic-like cells, appear to communicate with plasmablasts and fibroblasts. Cytokines shown near these cells include TGFβ, IL‑1β, IFNγ, and IL‑10, suggesting mixed inflammatory and profibrotic signaling. Plasmablasts also seem to promote fibroblast activation through factors such as PDGFβ and LOXL2.\n\nA central outcome is the conversion or activation of fibroblasts toward myofibroblasts, which is linked to collagen deposition. Macrophages marked as MERTK-positive are also included, apparently contributing through efferocytosis of apoptotic cells and secretion of TGFβ and IL‑10, further feeding fibroblast activation. Overall, the image conveys how coordinated interactions among B cells, T cells, macrophages, and stromal cells may drive fibrosis.",
